kosha lime-chemistry

  -- the oldest construction chemistry: lime cycle.
  -- used for 10,000 years — plaster, mortar, whitewash.
  -- the complete lime cycle:
  --
  --   1. CALCINATION (burning limestone):
  --      CaCO3 → CaO + CO2↑    (900°C, endothermic, 178 kJ/mol)
  --      CaO = quicklime. highly reactive. dangerous: reacts with water violently.
  --
  --   2. SLAKING (adding water):
  --      CaO + H2O → Ca(OH)2 + heat  (65 kJ/mol exothermic — "slaking heat")
  --      Ca(OH)2 = slaked lime / hydrated lime. white powder or putty.
  --      used as plaster, whitewash, mortar.
  --
  --   3. CARBONATION (setting and hardening):
  --      Ca(OH)2 + CO2 → CaCO3 + H2O  (absorbs CO2 from air)
  --      the plaster slowly converts back to calcium carbonate.
  --      this is the REVERSE of calcination — the cycle closes.
  --      lime plaster gets harder over years as carbonation continues.
  --      historical lime plasters are harder than modern ones (more carbonated).
  --
  -- NET CYCLE: CaCO3 → (burn) → CaO → (slake) → Ca(OH)2 → (carbonate) → CaCO3
  -- CO2 released in burning is reabsorbed in carbonation. NET CO2 = 0 (in theory).
  -- much lower CO2 than Portland cement.
  --
  -- hydraulic lime: contains silicates (from clay impurities in limestone).
  -- sets underwater via hydration (like cement) not just carbonation.
  -- used in marine and foundation work before Portland cement.

